    "product_id": "0d13f34f-9daa-407f-8abf-668234e05218",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 020730\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1230 AM PDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Above-normal temperatures and quiet conditions continue into \n  next week as high pressure dominates.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Thursday\n\nA slight zonal to ridging pattern becomes more of a pronounced \nridge over the weekend. This will continue our pattern of light \nwinds and warmer temperatures. The ridging pattern will fluctuate\nslightly over the next week leading to some slight warming and \ncooling, but temperatures will continue to be on the warmer side.\nExpect highs around 5 to 10 degrees above average (the mid to \nupper 90s for most of the desert valleys, getting near 110 for \nDeath Valley) and lows closer to 10 degrees above average (70s for\nmost areas and in the mid 80s at Death Valley). These conditions \nlast through the next work week with models hinting at a pattern \nchange arriving beyond the forecast period. \n\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds \nremain light and follow typical, daily patterns. Chance of seeing \n10+ knots is 10% or less throughout the TAF period. Clear skies \nuntil high clouds move in Friday evening.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light and diurnal wind \npatterns expected in most locations throughout the TAF period. \nBreezy conditions are forecast in the western Mojave Desert and \nsouthern Great Basin, but gusts should remain around 20 knots or \nless and generally be from a southerly or easterly direction. Clear \nskies overnight with high clouds moving in Friday afternoon and \nevening.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
